 We argue that reusable verification state should retain preparation while separating each candidate's execution history and lifetime. Preparing context before candidate evaluation makes that native state available to independent attempts. Each candidate inherits the prepared state, so preceding alternatives neither supply its solver history nor extend its lifetime. The benefit depends on preparation cost, branch creation cost and the pages that each candidate modifies.
 
-Realizing this separation requires runtime safety, faithful resource handling and bounded memory. A branch boundary must be quiescent and safe for the verifier runtime. Native configuration and resource accounting must remain faithful when process creation changes timers or descriptors~\cite{fork}. Preparation sharing must improve service capacity within a memory budget that bounds retained states. Together, these requirements connect compatibility to the service's resource frontier, the achievable combinations of latency, throughput and memory use at matched CPU capacity and memory limits.
+Realizing this separation requires runtime safety, faithful resource handling and bounded memory. The branch boundary must be quiescent and safe for the verifier runtime. Native configuration and resource accounting must remain faithful when process creation changes timers or descriptors~\cite{fork}. Sharing must also improve service capacity within a memory budget that bounds retained states. These requirements connect compatibility to the service's resource frontier: its achievable combinations of latency, throughput and memory use at matched CPU capacity and memory limits.
 
 We present a verification service whose parent retains immutable prepared states and leaves candidate verification to disposable independent branches. Each branch owns its responses, mutable state, communication and cancellation, inherits native resource state, and receives separate service accounting. Supported quiescent boundaries, untouched candidate operations after preparation and bounded prepared-state lifetimes address runtime compatibility and memory use. Unsupported boundaries use native execution. We compare complete verification on public Lean and SMT-backed candidate streams with competent bounded warm services and native logical or serialized prepared-state reuse. After preparation reuse, we assess the remaining heterogeneous checking on an RTX 5090 with setup, transfer and CPU fallback included in complete verification costs. \missing{Matched behavior results and end-to-end latency/throughput/memory findings, including the measured scope of device benefit.}
 
